Here is the complete, faithful English translation of your prompt, maintaining all technical requirements, architectural guidelines, structural constraints, and presentation readiness principles.

Project Context
This is a take-home assignment for the position of GenAI & Competitive Intelligence Engineer at JFrog. The evaluator is your direct hiring manager. The manager estimated that this task requires about two days of work.

The Goal: Production-quality code, thoroughly documented, with clear architectural reasoning-not "the maximum number of features," but mature engineering judgment.

The Assignment (JFrog Home Assignment)
Stage 1 - Competitive Intelligence Tool
A system that delivers daily updates on industry news and insights and compares JFrog against its main competitors.

Required Deliverables
A real working solution (not a mockup)

System architecture

A real UI

A clear separation between "built now" vs. "future capabilities"

Documentation of challenges and pitfalls

A README.md with execution instructions

Readiness to present the solution to a non-technical audience in English

Suggested Architecture (Keep it simple-do not overengineer)
Ingestion: RSS / Blogs / Release Notes for JFrog and 3-4 competitors, configured via a file list.

Daily Execution: GitHub Actions cron or a simple scheduler.

Processing: Deduplication, followed by an LLM that classifies and summarizes with structured output (Pydantic).

Relevance Scoring: The LLM rates each news item from 1 to 5 across several dimensions (e.g., direct relation to JFrog, competitor importance, strategic impact, freshness). The weighted score is calculated in code, not by the LLM, using weights specified in a configuration file. Dimension scores are stored so weights can be tuned without re-querying the LLM.

Comparison: Matrix comparing JFrog vs. competitors, with every claim hyperlinked to its source (never generated from model memory).

UI: Streamlit featuring:

Daily Digest (sorted by relevance score)

Weight Tuning Sliders

Comparison Tab

Storage: SQLite.

Security & Reliability
Scraped web content is treated as untrusted (risk of prompt injection) with clear isolation in the prompt.

Cost and rate-limiting controls.

Every claim must include a source link to mitigate hallucinations.

Key Presentation Point (Crucial-Include in README, DECISIONS.md, and Presentation Script)
In the future, users will not manually adjust weights. Instead, they will provide feedback or correct an item's rating with a brief rationale (e.g., "Not relevant because..."), and an automated feedback loop will ingest this feedback to continuously adjust weights (and potentially refine prompts) automatically-identical in principle to a lead scoring feedback loop.

Built Now (Small & Cost-Effective Infrastructure)
A feedback table in SQLite: item ID, original score, corrected score / 👍👎, optional text rationale, timestamp.

In the UI: 👍/👎 buttons and an optional rationale text input next to each news item.

The learning engine itself (automated weight adjustment) is documented as "Future Work" and is not implemented unless extra time remains.

Additional Future Work (To document in README under "Not Built")
Embeddings and Vector DB as data volume grows

Slack notifications

Classification evaluation test set (Eval suite)

Additional ingestion sources

Stage 2 - DevOps
Fork jfrog_task.

Scan with JFrog Xray.

Identify 2 applicable vulnerabilities.

Explain in your own words what contextual analysis is and why it shifts remediation priorities.

Fix one of the vulnerabilities.

Build a Docker image, push it to Artifactory, and publish Build Info using publish-build.yml.

Provide a step-by-step breakdown of every command used and what it does.

Approved Decisions for Stage 1
(Follow these strictly, changes require explicit confirmation)

Tracked Competitors (Configurable file list, not hardcoded)
Core: Sonatype, GitHub, GitLab, Snyk

Secondary / Optional: Cloudsmith, Harness

AWS & GCP Registries: Not tracked as discrete entities, referenced only as contextual background in comparison views.

Note: This list was established based on third-party aggregator research and represents a documented engineering judgment in DECISIONS.md.

Data Sources
Official blogs, release notes / changelogs, quarterly financial reports (for publicly traded JFrog and GitLab), security research blogs, DevOps news outlets, pricing pages (to detect changes), and open job postings as strategic signals.
Requirement: Verify all feed URLs, do not invent mock URLs.

Hallucination Prevention
Every claim in a news item or comparison matrix must include a link to its source and a brief quote. If no source exists, display "Unknown" rather than generating claims from model memory. The comparison matrix is constructed exclusively from official product pages.

Live Demo Capabilities
A "Run Now" button triggering live ingestion.

Cron execution history to demonstrate automated background runs.

A pre-populated SQLite database with a few days of history (seed data) to ensure the demo functions seamlessly even if the internet or a target site goes down.

LLM Integration
Model name and provider must reside strictly in configuration files (allowing one-line model switching). Final model selection and cost tradeoffs are evaluated separately. Design the system to ensure seamless provider swapping.

Streamlit UI - JFrog Design System
Tone & Aesthetic: High-tech, dark, modern.

Design tokens were estimated from official site screenshots (place these in a centralized CSS variables / theme file).

Color Palette
Page Background: #070B19 (Deep Navy, not pure black)

Elevated Surface / Card: #0A0F21

Top Bar: #080C25

Brand Green: #40BE46 (Links, accents, active states)

Button / Active Tab Green: #36A13B

Highlighted Header Green: #66BB56

Inactive Tab: #1B2147 (Dark Indigo)

Tiles / Icons: #333642

Glassmorphism Panel: #14243D to #1E2D3E

Category & Status Badges: Blue #0157A0, Purple #8603A3, Dark Green #025803

Text: White #FFFFFF, Secondary Text: Slate-Gray #8C9FA4

Top Banner Gradient: Green #3EAF57 → Blue #21558A

Form & Feel
Pill-shaped buttons and tabs (border-radius: 9999px). Primary: solid green, Secondary: thin white outline.

Cards: Large border radius (24-28px), 1px slate-gray border, semi-transparent background ("glassmorphism"), subtle depth layering.

Small, colorful chips/badges for status, competitor, and score. JFrog is always green, each competitor gets a dedicated accent color.

Dual-color main header (half green, half white). Green hyperlinks with trailing chevron (›).

Subtle green glow around key KPIs or high scores. KPI cards present a large number with a small descriptive label underneath.

Relevance score presented as a ring or glowing bar rather than plain text.

Clean background with a subtle mesh/gradient pattern. Do not copy official logos, illustrations, or graphics.

Subtle animations only (hover, fade-in)-no flickering or visual noise.

Typography
Google Font Open Sans. Headings in 600-700 weight, body in 400, structured scale hierarchy.

Streamlit Implementation
.streamlit/config.toml configuring dark mode theme parameters (backgroundColor, secondaryBackgroundColor, primaryColor, textColor, font).

Centralized CSS file (styles.css) loaded once. Cards, badges, and score rings rendered via safe HTML using st.markdown(unsafe_allow_html=True).

Pill-tab styling in Streamlit via CSS can be fragile across versions. Pin the Streamlit version and document it clearly in the README.md.

Dark theme only. Ensure high text contrast (no green text on green backgrounds).

Explicitly state in README.md that components emulate the JFrog look-and-feel rather than using an official public component library.

Structure: Daily Digest Tab (sorted by score, weight sliders, 👍/👎 feedback with text) and Comparison Tab.

Clean, spacious, and legible. Prioritize clarity over complex visual effects.

Model Context Protocol (MCP) Integration Strategy
Ingestion Pipeline
Do not use MCP inside the main ingestion pipeline, standard Python code is simpler and more robust.

Optional Bonus Capability
Once core functionality is solid, build a small, read-only MCP server exposing three tools over the underlying SQLite database:

search_news

get_comparison

get_digest

This allows CI team members to query data in natural language from their own native tools (e.g., Cursor, Claude Desktop). To support this, decouple business logic into a dedicated service layer separate from the Streamlit UI.

General Engineering Guidelines
(Apply at all times)

Simplicity First: Avoid Docker/Kubernetes/microservices/message queues unless explicitly required by the task. (Note: Docker is explicitly required for Stage 2). Over-engineering indicates poor judgment rather than technical skill.

Justify Every Architectural Decision: When evaluating multiple approaches (e.g., TF-IDF vs. Embeddings/Vector DBs, or orchestration frameworks), briefly document trade-offs before implementation and select a default based on problem scale.

Rigorous Error Handling: Every external API, network call, or file operation requires targeted try/except blocks, clear non-sensitive user error messages (no raw stack traces exposed to end-users), and reasonable fallback mechanisms.

Readable Code Over "Clever" Code: Clear naming conventions, small focused functions, and no complex one-liners written just to impress.

Security Requirements
Zero Hardcoded Secrets/API Keys: Use .env files exclusively and confirm .gitignore blocks them prior to committing. Include an .env.example template containing dummy placeholder values.

Input Validation: Validate all external data (files, APIs, web scrape outputs) before passing it into business logic.

Prompt Injection Defense: Isolate untrusted web content injected into LLM prompts using explicit delimiters separating system instructions from retrieved text. Document your awareness of prompt injection risks in code comments and the README.md.

Cost & Execution Guardrails: Every loop or agent execution graph must implement strict max_iterations, max_depth, or execution timeouts to prevent runaway API spend.

PII Handling: Do not store Personally Identifiable Information in plain text.

Enterprise Security References: Mention in the README.md that a production environment would utilize tools like JFrog Xray/Curation for dependency vulnerability scanning and a JFrog MCP Registry to verify external MCP tools/servers before deployment.

Repository Structure & Documentation Standards
Maintain a clean Git history with descriptive atomic commits (no single massive final commit).

Required README.md Sections:

Complete setup and execution instructions (including dependency installation and required environment variables).

Primary architectural decisions and rationale (the "why" is more important than the "what").

A concise Security Considerations section (3-5 lines) detailing implemented controls and future hardening steps.

Honest documentation of known limitations and features you would build with more time.

Include unit tests for critical business logic (e.g., weighted relevance score calculation and deduplication).

MCP Evaluation & Decision Protocol
Utilizing Existing MCP Servers
If a functional need can be solved using an existing MCP server (e.g., web search, external database integration), prefer MCP over custom code if it does not introduce unnecessary operational complexity.

Before choosing an MCP server, evaluate:

Which MCP server is proposed?

What specific value does it add over native code?

How does it align with treating MCP tools as vetted enterprise assets (analogous to the JFrog MCP Registry)?

Document all MCP adoption decisions in DECISIONS.md.

Building Custom MCP Servers
Because the job description highlights proficiency in building MCPs and Skills, evaluate custom MCP development before writing code:

Determine if a solution component can be exposed as an independent MCP tool protocol server, decoupling logic from the agent graph for cross-client reusability (Cursor, Claude Desktop, auxiliary agents).

Suitable candidates include self-contained capabilities like knowledge base retrieval, lead scoring, or company enrichment.

Document this evaluation explicitly before implementation: "I propose wrapping X as an MCP server because Y, compared to a standard Python module." Document the final choice (and reasons if rejected) in DECISIONS.md.

Architectural Decision Log (DECISIONS.md)
Maintain a DECISIONS.md file at the root of the project. Whenever a key decision is made-architectural, library selection, workflow pivot, or non-trivial fix-update this file immediately.

Entry Format (Chronological, newest at the top):
Markdown
## [Date/Stage] Short Title of Decision

**Selected Option:** Concise summary of chosen approach

**Alternatives Considered:** Other options evaluated and why they were rejected

**Rationale:** Primary driver (Performance, Simplicity, Scale, Cost/Latency, Security)

**How to Explain in an Interview (20-30 Seconds Verbal):**
> Concise, spoken response tailored for an interviewer

**JFrog Product Connection (If applicable):**
How a production JFrog product (Xray / Curation / MCP Registry / AppTrust) would serve this need in an enterprise setting (only include genuine, logical connections).
Panel Presentation & Interview Readiness
The next stage requires presenting this solution to a 4-person technical panel. You must be prepared to confidently explain the architecture, tool selections, trade-offs, and future improvements.

Every library and module requires a 1-2 sentence rationale, including one rejected alternative.

Minimize Abstraction: Avoid complex design patterns unless strictly necessary.

Lean Dependencies: Every new dependency must be justified.

Code Quality: Use type hints and concise docstrings. Comments must explain why, not what.

Presentation Preparation File (PRESENTATION_PREP.md): Generated upon task completion, including:

A 7-10 minute presentation script (Problem, Strategy, Architecture, Key Decisions).

A live demo outline and contingency plan for failure scenarios.

8-10 anticipated panel questions (Architecture, Security, Scaling, Costs) with concise answers.

3-4 honest "What I would improve with more time" points.

Genuine connection points to the JFrog product ecosystem.

Scope Discipline
Execute the exact scope requested. Do not add arbitrary "bonus" features without explicit approval.

Ask clarifying questions when requirements are ambiguous rather than making unverified assumptions.

Prioritize a fully functional, clean, well-documented deliverable over an ambitious, unfinished one.

